FINDING TYPE CLASSIFICATION
Produce the finding first from the evidence. Classify its supported claims with the vocabulary below; this vocabulary does not limit the issues you may report. Missing or uncertain type metadata never removes a finding or changes ERROR, WARNING, SUGGESTION or INFO.
Each comment, candidate finding and cross_cutting_concerns entry includes:
"finding_classification": { "types": ["<one or more supported core slugs and any supported custom slugs>"], "level": "statement|member|type|file|cross_file", "qualifier": "missing|incorrect|extraneous", "confidence": 0.0 }.
When an ancillary level, qualifier or classification-confidence axis is unassessed, return null for that axis and retain the supported types. Unknown ancillary axes never require replacing known types.
Use the highest code level the finding itself concerns, independently of its anchor or risk. The qualifier describes whether the code concerned is absent, wrong, or unnecessary. Classification confidence is a number from 0 to 1 describing the assigned types and axes; it is independent of correctness confidence in risk_assessment. Do not invent a type or send custom IDs, taxonomy versions, or provenance.
Preserve classifications for unchanged source claims. For a combined claim retain its supported source types. A materially rewritten claim may use its own evidence-supported classification. Missing rewrite metadata must not erase supported source types. Keep the claim no stronger than its source findings.
